  "intro": "When the population standard deviation is unknown, a confidence interval for the mean uses the t-distribution: $\\bar{x}\\pm t^*\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$, where $s$ is the sample standard deviation and $t^*$ comes from $n-1$ degrees of freedom. The t-distribution is bell-shaped but has heavier tails than the normal; as $n$ grows it approaches the normal. The standard error of the mean is $\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$. Conditions: random sample, independence, and an approximately normal population or a large enough sample.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "For $\\bar{x}=50$, $s=10$, $n=25$, find the standard error of the mean.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "SE of the mean is $\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$.",
          "$\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{25}}=\\frac{10}{5}=2$.",
          "So SE $=2$."
        ],
        "answer": "SE $=2$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "How many degrees of freedom for a one-sample t interval with $n=25$?",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Degrees of freedom are $n-1$.",
          "$25-1=24$."
        ],
        "answer": "$24$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Build the interval for $\\bar{x}=50$, SE $=2$, $t^*=2.064$ (df $=24$, $95\\%$).",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Margin $=t^*\\cdot\\text{SE}=2.064\\cdot 2=4.128$.",
          "Interval: $50\\pm 4.128$.",
          "Endpoints: about $(45.9,\\,54.1)$."
        ],
        "answer": "About $(45.9,\\,54.1)$"
      }
    },
